def candidate(token_ids, table, gamma, beta, w_in, w_out):
    tokens = table[token_ids]
    centered = tokens - jnp.mean(tokens, axis=-1, keepdims=True)
    spread = jnp.sqrt(jnp.mean(centered**2, axis=-1, keepdims=True) + 1e-5)
    normalized = centered / spread * gamma + beta
    projected = normalized @ w_in
    activated = jax.nn.gelu(projected)
    hidden = activated @ w_out
    return tokens + hidden
--- attempt 2: correct, speedup 86.993x (reference 60.038 ms, candidate 0.690 ms)
rationale: Hypothesis: fuse row normalization into the first projection by using the identity (centered / spread * gamma + beta) @ w_in = ((tokens - mean) / spread * gamma) @ w_in + beta @ w_in. This removes the full normalized intermediate and can reduce memory traffic while preserving both dense projections. Expected HLO/profile difference: normalization feeds the projection more directly, with beta projected once and broadcast across rows; inspect for fewer elementwise kernels or reduced intermediate traffic. Correctness risk: algebraic reassociation changes floating-point rounding, so accept only if measurement against the reference stays within tolerance. Use block_until_ready when timing asynchronous GPU work.
